NAME
    web-analyze - passive Chromium/CDP website evidence and Markdown analysis

SYNOPSIS
    web-analyze <http(s)-url> <analysis-path> [--scroll]

DESCRIPTION
    Launches a fresh headless Chromium session through the existing native-CDP
    web-analyze.lib.js recorder. The command waits for a rendered document,
    waits for a heuristic document-settled condition before the initial
    full rendered-DOM snapshot, then acquires a read-only deep-rest phase
    (CDP DOM tree including Shadow DOM, layout/style snapshots, native MHTML,
    CSSOM/style source, initial-navigation CSS rule-usage coverage,
    per-node resolved/matched CSS and platform fonts,
    Accessibility trees with DOM backend identities,
    runtime element/storage details, listener locations,
    frame targets, redacted network event metadata and archived responses).
    It inventories controls/forms/headings and best-effort React runtime
    markers without issuing user input. With the optional --scroll operand,
    it then scrolls the document and nested scroll containers, recording
    additional bounded states after settlement checks.
    It then stops Chromium,
    allowing the recorder's normal network/resource drain, and generates
    analytical Markdown documents from the acquired evidence.

    This is observation, not autonomous proof of every application behavior.
    The command does not click buttons, submit forms, authenticate, follow
    links, modify account state, bypass challenges or claim unseen states.
    Even an optional scroll sweep cannot exhaust a dynamic React app.

OPERANDS
    http(s)-url       Required absolute HTTP or HTTPS URL to navigate.
    analysis-path     Parent folder for analysis runs. Created if missing.
                      Each invocation creates a unique private run-* folder;
                      pre-existing analyses are preserved unchanged.
    --scroll          Optional: perform passive automated scrolling after
                      completing the initial deep resting-page capture.
                      Default: no programmatic scroll or user input.

OUTPUT
    Prints the absolute path to the generated README.md on stdout.
    Progress and diagnostics go to stderr.

    Inside each run-* folder:
      README.md        Analysis index, evidence counts, confidentiality notice
      DOM.md           Observed routes, headings, forms and interactive controls
      NETWORK.md       Request catalog (bounded summary), resource outcomes
      SCRIPTS.md       Compiled JS/Wasm archive kind, source-map provenance
                       and best-effort React/Next evidence
      FLOWS.md         Mermaid diagram and table of observed scroll transitions
      LIMITATIONS.md   Coverage limits, missing resource outcomes and warnings
      evidence-integrity.json
                       Verified file/digest/size results, per-domain/target
                       outcomes and post-capture discrepancy identities
      rest/css-rule-usage/index.json
                       Start/stop timestamps, target coverage, used/unused
                       recorded rule-use totals, incomplete/unsupported tracking
      rest/css-rule-usage/target-NNN.json
                       Private CDP rule-usage entries (used flags) per target
      rest/target-graph-initial.json
                       Discovered/attached target and execution contexts
      rest/target-graph-final.json
                       Refreshed target hierarchy and unresolved attachments
      rest/dom-history-contexts.json
                       Instrumentation health, context/frame IDs and loss flags
      rest/dom-mutations-at-rest.jsonl
                       Private chronological mutation data before scrolling
      rest/            Rest-phase indexed evidence, cookies, per-target
                       local/session/IndexedDB/Cache Storage, DOM/layout/CSS,
                       bounded per-node matched/computed styles, CSS variables,
                       platform fonts and per-frame Accessibility trees with support/
                       failure outcomes, listeners, runtime environment and
                       native MHTML
      rest/security/index.json
                       Private cross-reference of observed frame security
                       origins, secure/isolation context and Network response
                       TLS details; version-gated HTTPS Network.getCertificate
                       results are retained separately under rest/security/.
                       HTTP does not imply a TLS chain; unsupported commands,
                       missing observations and bounds remain explicit.
      rest/optional-domains/index.json
                       Version-specific optional CDP domain capability report:
                       installed protocol methods, observed wire events and
                       already enabled targets for animation, media, audio,
                       WebRTC, tracing, background and reporting domains.
                       Enables only passive Animation/Media event notifications
                       on page/iframe targets when supported. Does not alter
                       playback or timing, run tracing or profile the page;
                       schema availability is not counted as captured data.
      rest/worker-provenance/index.json
                       Private offline correlation of native CDP target/frame
                       lifetime events, worker script versions, service-worker
                       registrations and observed default execution contexts.
                       ServiceWorker.enable subscribes to updates only when the
                       installed browser protocol supports it; no start/update,
                       unregister or network fetch is triggered. Missing OOPIFs
                       or unattached targets are not treated as complete.
      rest/pwa-resources/index.json
                       Private, read-only manifest/resource provenance: exact
                       observed manifest, start URL and icon references matched
                       to already archived browser Network responses, bodies
                       and frame resource tree. No fetches or install attempts;
                       not-observed references and worker scope remain explicit.
                       Correlation is by exact URL, not proof of shared request ID.
      rest/targets/target-NNN/frame-context.json
                       Private per-frame storage keys, permission policy,
                       origin trials, isolation, resource tree, PWA manifest
                       and per-frame bucket names. Secondary distinct keys
                       are queried in observed default execution contexts;
                       unavailable/ambiguous context identity stays partial,
                       with no implicit bucket or isolated-world creation.
      rest/targets/target-NNN/storage-partitions/index.json
                       Bounded per-storageKey/default-or-named-bucket selection
                       outcomes, distinct from legacy origin-scoped archives.
      rest/targets/target-NNN/storage-partitions/selection-NNN/
                       Scoped IndexedDB records, Cache Storage metadata/body
                       bytes and read-only CDP OPFS directory listings. File
                       content bytes are not recovered from OPFS. Method/argument
                       support is checked against the installed browser's
                       /json/protocol; missing or truncated evidence is partial.
      states/          Per-state rendered HTML/text/controls in JSON, selected PNGs
                       dom-rest-initial.html / dom-rest-final.html (full outerHTML)
                       dom-rest-{initial,final}.json (settlement evidence)
      cdp-capabilities.json  Browser version, schema discovery status,
                             supported domains/commands/events
      cdp-protocol.json      Version-specific native Chromium CDP schema;
                             only present when discovery succeeds
      cdp-wire.jsonl         Every CDP packet observed by the connected
                             debugger, including unhandled events and results
      dom-mutations.jsonl    Private detailed mutation chronology: added and
                             removed nodes, text and attribute changes,
                             Shadow DOM roots, execution contexts and frames
      network-details.jsonl  Private, full CDP HTTP/request and live-message details
      resources.jsonl  CDP response outcome index, including redirects,
                       recovery source, failures and incomplete streams
      resources/       Complete response bodies and named .partial artifacts,
                       all indexed with SHA-256 and size
      scripts.jsonl    Debugger.scriptParsed language/context/provenance and
                       JavaScript or Wasm bytecode/source outcomes
      scripts/         Content-addressed JavaScript source, WebAssembly
                       binary bytecode or explicitly labelled disassembly
      rest/source-maps/index.json
                       Rest-boundary source map references and limitations
      rest/source-maps/<sha>.bin
                       Original recovered map bytes from inline data or
                       responses already archived by native CDP
      *.jsonl          Lifecycle, DOM, interaction, network and runtime events
      summary.json     Recording totals and incomplete-resource counters
      profile/         Dedicated Chromium browsing profile for this run

    The detailed document-start DOM observer also records node IDs stable
    inside each JavaScript context, insertion/removal history (even for
    nodes absent at final snapshot), callback-time markup/text and attribute
    old/current values. A supported attachShadow interception observes new
    open and closed roots, including nested ones, but changes browser method
    identity and cannot capture closed roots created before installation,
    missed early instrumentation or every intermediate synchronous value.
    Markup/text snapshots exceeding 256 Ki UTF-16 code units are clipped
    with explicit flags, not silently treated as complete. These archives
    are confidential and are not copied to privacy-redacted dom.jsonl.

    DOM quiet observation also watches discovered open Shadow DOM trees and
    readable same-origin iframe documents, but inaccessible frames cannot
    be treated as completely observed.

    A DOM snapshot is considered settled when document.readyState is
    complete, document fonts are loaded, DOM mutations and network events
    have been quiet for 1,500 ms, and no HTTP requests or body reads remain
    in flight. If requests remain open after 4,500 ms without a network
    event and DOM mutations are quiet, the snapshot is saved as
    dom-quiet-network-pending rather than settled. This avoids indefinite
    waits on persistent streams while reporting unresolved network work.
    Initial settlement is allowed up to 15 seconds; subsequent states
    up to 6 seconds, within an overall 45-second exploration budget.
    These are heuristics, not proof of final application state.
    If settlement times out (e.g. continuous DOM updates), the command
    still saves best-effort DOM evidence with status timeout/unavailable.
    The rest phase captures observed browser cookies (including HttpOnly)
    and readable localStorage/sessionStorage values. It freezes a copy of the
    private CDP network detail stream at this point. Values may include active
    credentials; do not transmit the output or publish it to GitHub.
    Deep resting-page evidence is captured before any programmatic scroll.
    The default command stops at this boundary; --scroll enables an additional
    exploration phase with a separate time budget.
    The existing bounded scroll sweep still follows as a separate operation.
    Rest artifact statuses now propagate individual listener, stylesheet,
    local/session storage and interrupted-capture failures. After Chromium
    exits, the command validates the evidence root against file contents,
    expected SHA-256 and byte lengths, nested IndexedDB/cache/CSS/AX indexes,
    DOM/CDP wire event counts, dropped logs and incomplete target coverage.
    The private evidence-integrity.json reports saved/partial/failed/
    interrupted/inapplicable/unsupported per domain and target. Readable
    reports show only summary integrity statuses, not cookies, raw query
    parameters, page text or other private browser content. Incomplete or
    damaged evidence does not make the command claim complete acquisition.
    A REST status of partial must be inspected rather than interpreted as
    proof of complete source acquisition. Full initial/final rendered HTML
    is stored separately from bounded exploratory JSON snapshots.

    The rest collector also inventories compiled-script source map references
    and resolves only data: maps or source maps already captured as complete
    network response bodies. It performs no extra map downloads and cannot
    certify maps never loaded by the page/browser. Source-map v3 payloads
    retain sourceRoot, mappings, names and embedded sourcesContent in the
    original bytes; index entries expose bounded source counts, original
    script URL, full digest and clear unavailable/partial outcomes.
    All source-map and Wasm binary files are private and independently audited.

    The command opts into Chromium CSS rule-usage tracking before initial
    navigation for available attached page/iframe targets. Tracking stops
    when rest capture begins, before later deep CSS inspection and before
    any optional --scroll. The rest/css-rule-usage/ archive retains exact
    CDP rule-use ranges, per-target timestamps, observed-use counts and
    unsupported/incomplete target outcomes. It is bounded to 64 targets
    or 20 seconds. The result is coverage over an observed interval, not
    a denominator of all possible CSS rules across future states. An
    absent rule is not proven unused; only explicit CDP used=false entries
    count as reported-unused. CSS
    tracking has profiling overhead; it never synthesizes page input.

    Deep CSS inspection stores per-target css-nodes/index.json and
    css-nodes/page-NNN.json with real matched CSS rules/pseudo elements,
    computed properties including custom properties, and available platform
    fonts. The browser schema gates each method; no unsupported feature is
    claimed. The pass is bounded to 384 observed DOM elements and 20 seconds
    per target, with explicit skipped/failed/unsupported method counts and
    independently audited raw file digests. It does not simulate :hover,
    :focus or other user-controlled CSS states.

    The bounded exploration captures at most 29 states, with up to 1,200
    controls and 2 MB rendered HTML per-state JSON. Its final Markdown tables
    summarize a bounded prefix; the raw indexes retain all indexed outcomes.
    Recursively attached iframe/worker targets are inventoried with their
    parent target, execution contexts, enabled instrumentation domains and
    errors. Worker globals have separate snapshots instead of page DOM/CSS.
    Chromium can render cross-origin frames in one process or a separate
    OOPIF, so frame tree and target graph remain distinct evidence.

    The security inventory never overrides certificate verification, installs
    trust authorities or enables Security tracking. Network.getCertificate is
    used only for observed HTTPS origins when supported; security fields are
    browser-reported observations rather than independently established trust.
    Private certificate bytes, hostnames and response data are not copied into
    Markdown reports.

    The raw packet ledger does not filter by known CDP event names; it
    includes outgoing commands and incoming responses/notifications while
    the debugger is connected. The summary reports packets and local write
    errors. A complete ledger of *observed* packets is not a guarantee that
    disabled CDP domains, unattached targets or future activity were observed.
    The protocol schema is retrieved from this browser's local debugging
    endpoint and is not assumed to be identical to a remote specification.
    A missing schema is recorded as unavailable, not silently substituted.

    Per-target CDP response buffering and durable-message configuration
    are attempted when available; their actual results, not assumptions,
    are preserved in summary.json resourceCapture.networkConfiguration.
    Completed responses use Network.getResponseBody first, followed by
    read-only streamed response or frame-resource lookup where supported.
    Only EventSource and text/event-stream responses are candidates for
    progressive stream capture. A partial response is never labeled saved
    as a full body. Each redirect hop is indexed with the next URL; raw
    request/response events remain in the private CDP wire ledger.
    Stream memory is capped at 16 MiB per request and 64 MiB total.
    Resource capture remains best effort: redirects, active streams,
    unsupported commands, process swaps and evictions can prevent a
    complete body. Inspect per-request statuses and limits.

ENVIRONMENT
    Requires an initialized m environment (nonempty m_ROOT and m_LIB_DIR),
    Node.js >= 22 (native WebSocket) and chromium on effective PATH.
    From a clean shell invoke through: m web-analyze <url> <analysis-path>.
    Browser sandbox and TLS verification remain enabled; no Playwright needed.

SECURITY
    Private run directories and report/evidence files are created with
    restrictive permissions. Raw URLs, HTML, JS, response bodies, browser
    profile, HTTP headers, cookies, browser storage and screenshots may contain
    credentials, personal information
    or signed URLs. Do not commit or share output without review.
    CDP is bound to 127.0.0.1 with an ephemeral port and retains local
    debugger-level access during execution.

EXIT STATUS
    0   Completed and at least one rendered DOM state was observed.
    1   m environment is missing.
    2   Invalid/missing operands.
    3   Invalid URL.
    4   Unsupported URL scheme.
    5   Could not allocate a unique run directory.
    6   m library root unavailable.
    7   Existing web-analyze CDP recorder unavailable.
    8   Chromium capture initialization failed.
    9   Recorder finalization failed.
    10  Markdown report generation failed.
    11  No rendered DOM state observed (inspect partial evidence).
    12  Unexpected execution failure.

EXAMPLE
    m web-analyze https://example.org ./analysis

